Interesting way of stating it, and true in principle.  However,
pudding farming is order (s?) of magnitude different from regular
level camping for a couple of reasons.  First (and perhaps most
importantly) it is self-sustaining, there is always enough food
because you can eat the pudding corpses until the food ration drops
start to accumulate.  Secondly, the number of death drops per turn is
much greater since every pudding kill leaves a corpse and therefore
has a possibility of leaving a death drop and you're scoring a kill
every few turns.
Given the degree of differences I'm not sure "lesser instances" work
since if the lesser instances fall below a certain threshold, say by
not providing enough food, then they don't really work.  Maybe at high
enough level when the types of monsters generated leave enough food to
sustain the player, but then we probably aren't talking about a
character who needs to altar camp.


